    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-12-3289-2012",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Uptake and removal of soluble trace gases and aerosols by precipitation represents a major uncertainty in the processes that control the vertical distribution of atmospheric trace species. Model representations of precipitation scavenging vary greatly in their complexity, and most are divorced from the physics of precipitation formation and transformation. Here, we describe a new large-scale precipitation scavenging algorithm, developed for the UCI chemistry-transport model (UCI-CTM), that represents a step toward a more physical treatment of scavenging through improvements in the formulation of the removal in sub-gridscale cloudy and ambient environments and their overlap within the column as well as ice phase uptake of soluble species. The UCI algorithm doubles the lifetime of HNO3 in the upper troposphere relative to a scheme with commonly used fractional cloud cover assumptions and ice uptake determined by Henry's Law and provides better agreement with HNO3 observations. We find that the process of ice phase scavenging of HNO3 is a critical component of the tropospheric O3 budget, but that NOx and O3 mixing ratios are relatively insensitive to large differences in the removal rate. Ozone abundances are much more sensitive to the lifetime of HNO4, highlighting the need for better understanding of its interactions with ice and for additional observational constraints.",
    "field": "Atmospheric chemistry modeling",
    "problem": "Existing global chemistry models inaccurately represent precipitation scavenging of trace gases due to overly simplified treatments of sub-grid cloud overlap and ice-phase physics, which represents a major uncertainty in modeling the vertical distribution of atmospheric species like ozone.",
    "method": "The paper describes a new large-scale precipitation scavenging algorithm for global chemistry-transport models that provides a more physical treatment of removal by improving the formulation of sub-gridscale cloud overlap and the physics of ice phase uptake for soluble species.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a new precipitation scavenging algorithm for global chemistry models that incorporates more physical treatments of cloud overlap and ice physics, and to evaluate its impact on the chemical composition of the troposphere, particularly ozone.",
